{{c|{{smaller|Attic of St. Peter’s, Rome.<br>''A'', attic of the main edifice; ''B'', attic of the dome.}}}}
''Attic''<sup>1</sup>, and extract below.] '''1.''' In ''arch.'', a low story surmounting an entablature or the main cornice of a building. Also called ''attic story''.
{{smaller block|The term [''attic''] appears to have been introduced by the architects of the seventeenth century, with the intention of conveying [falsely] the idea that the feature to which it alluded was constructed or designed in the Athenian manner.
{{right|''Audsley'', Dict. of Architecture.}}}}
'''2.''' A room in the uppermost part of a house, immediately beneath the roof or leads; a garret.
{{smaller block|They stare not on the stars from out their ''attics''.
{{right|''Byron'', Beppo, st. 78.}}}}

